---
import Sheet from "../layouts/Sheet.astro";
import bundle from "../data/bundle.json";

const live = bundle.companies.filter((c) => !c.missing);
const betaMeasured = live.some((c) => c.results.method?.beta_measured);
const pill = { text: betaMeasured ? "beta fitted" : "beta ASSERTED, not fitted", tone: betaMeasured ? "good" : "open" };
---

<Sheet title="Method" system="06 · the gate — what is measured and what is merely built" sheet="06" pill={pill}>
  <div class="panel" style="border-color:var(--serious)">
    <h2>The headline, stated plainly</h2>
    <p class="sub">this is the most important sentence on the site</p>
    <p style="margin:0;max-width:92ch;font-size:15px">
      <b>No skill number on this site has been measured.</b> There is no backtest, the lambda beta
      is asserted rather than fitted, and the forecast intervals are uncalibrated. This is not an
      oversight to be filled in later — for two of the three it is <em>unobtainable</em> with the
      data available, and saying so is more useful than a number nobody should trust.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Why the backtest cannot run</h2>
      <p class="sub">the honest version</p>
      <p class="secondary" style="margin:0">
        Scoring our own estimate against past actuals is possible — the filings carry them. Scoring
        it against <em>the Street's bar as it stood at those quarters</em> is not: no source
        available to this build publishes consensus history. Without that, skill against consensus,
        the lambda regression and the per-regime beta table are all unobtainable rather than merely
        unmeasured. The market adapter that could serve current consensus was written, tested, and
        then deliberately refused to answer a historical as-of date, because returning today's
        estimates for a past quarter is lookahead that shows up as skill.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Why the intervals are uncalibrated</h2>
      <p class="sub">and what that means for reading them</p>
      <p class="secondary" style="margin:0">
        Calibration answers one question: of the times we said p10 to p90, how often did reality
        land inside? Answering it needs stored residuals from previous runs, and there are none —
        this is the first. So the bands on the Forecast sheet are the judge's own opinion of its
        uncertainty, and have never been checked against an outcome. Without that check a
        distribution is decorative, which is exactly why it is labelled instead of charted against a
        diagonal that would imply it had been tested.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h2>The ablation that would decide which lenses earn their tokens</h2>
    <p class="sub">not run — and this is the only acceptable basis for dropping a lens</p>
    <p class="secondary" style="margin:0 0 12px;max-width:92ch">
      Leave one lens out, re-run the judge against cached lens outputs, and record the change in
      error. A lens whose removal does not degrade the score is droppable on evidence. It costs one
      deep call per lens and needs an error to measure against — which loops back to the backtest.
      Until it runs, the nine-lens roster is a design claim rather than a measured one, and the two
      lenses that abstained most often on this run are the obvious first candidates.
    </p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>company</th><th class="num">lenses run</th><th class="num">survived V1</th><th class="num">dropped</th><th class="num">verification rate</th></tr>
      {live.map((c) => (
        <tr>
          <td><b>{c.ticker}</b></td>
          <td class="num">{c.results.lenses?.counts?.ran ?? 0}</td>
          <td class="num">{(c.results.reconciliation?.surviving ?? []).length}</td>
          <td class="num">{(c.results.reconciliation?.dropped ?? []).length}</td>
          <td class="num">{((c.results.reconciliation?.verification_rate ?? 0) * 100).toFixed(0)}%</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h2>Prompt versions used on this run</h2>
    <p class="sub">recorded so a future number can be traced to the text that produced it</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <tr><th>agent</th><th class="num">version</th></tr>
        {Object.entries(live[0]?.results?.method?.prompt_versions ?? {}).map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr><td class="mono">{k}</td><td class="num">{v}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</Sheet>
